    visualBrandingNotes:
      'Old Wall Street firm identity, not a faction crest and not a contemporary financial-services startup mark. Oxblood primary, warm cream secondary, brass-gold accent, forest-green reserved for active-rivalry state cues (contested-bid badges, live interrupt markers, reassignment notices) — never used as ambient color. The hero element is a single stylized uppercase "A" whose negative space resolves into a club-suit silhouette — the letterform is the mark; the club is the negative shape cut into it. Double-duty: "A" for Ashford, ace of clubs, family initial. Wordmark "ASHFORD CAPITAL PARTNERS" set in classic finance-firm serif with tight letterspacing and letterpress-deboss feel, oxblood on warm cream. Secondary sub-line "LICENSED CLEARANCE SERVICES · NEW YORK" in smaller uppercase sans-serif beneath — the intentional mismatch between the legacy-firm wordmark and the post-rift business line is the same awkward seam Golden Phoenix and Meridian both carry. The Ashford & Dane parent brand (not the ACP subsidiary) appears on correspondence letterhead, engraved stationery, and signet-ring heraldry. Material feel: engraved cream cardstock, letterpress deboss, brass foil stamp, oxblood leather ledger binding, walnut panel, 1890s mahogany card table patina. The club-suit motif should read as inherited Gilded-Age gentleman\'s-club heraldry, not as a casino logo. Reject: shield or crest silhouettes, gold-leaf flourish (that lane is Golden Phoenix), navy and silver (Meridian lane), matte-black and signal-cyan (A-List lane), casino-floor imagery, neon, gradient abuse, broadcast or tech-startup typography, calligraphy-as-ornament, fantasy heraldry, literal poker-chip textures, Las Vegas color palette, club glyphs drawn as playing-card faces rather than heraldic devices.',
