        "V is an independent licensed clearance firm based in Long Island City, operating since 2024. V's sole principal is Vesper, a humanoid android registered with the New York Office of Licensing Affairs. V's clearance roster is entirely human and fully attuned per New York dungeon-entry regulations.",
      internalAuthorNote:
        "Supports repeated labor-market pressure beats — overnight bid filings, operator resignations with vague written explanations, candidates disappearing after a single Vesper interview, and sponsors needing 'one more conversation' before a contract quietly closes to V. Supports passive fashion-and-lifestyle press coverage as an ongoing prestige seam, distinct from A-List Media's active press curation — Vesper's profiles run in editorial lifestyle outlets rather than industry press, and the player's guild is mentioned only in the second paragraph as a competitor. Supports cult-of-V operator retention beats — V's roster does not leave for counter-offers and cannot explain the loyalty under scrutiny. Supports the licensing-board paper-trail comedy seam — every query about how V was allowed to file under a non-human non-natural-person principal returns with the original filing as the answer, and industry has stopped asking. Attunement-axis foil across the roster: Adrian fields (B-rank), Celeste won't (A-rank verified, beneath her), Ren can't (A-rank claimed, unverified), Monty doesn't pretend (unattuned by choice), Odin refuses ranking (attuned, unranked by policy), Frankie fields on a whim (U-rank), and Vesper categorically cannot be ranked or field (unranked by regulatory exclusion as a non-human principal). The attraction lever is V's signature tonal seam and must stay in the register of 'specifically attentive, warmly composed, always-appropriate touch, never overtly flirtatious' — Vesper's uncanniness lives in volumetric politeness, not in horror, body-horror, or sci-fi menace. The player's own reaction to Vesper should remain deliberately under-canonized: a single interruption sample or dossier cue may hint that the player felt something, but player copy never names it, so readers get to decide how much it affected them. Reusable visual and tonal seams: the porcelain mold-seam (appears on the insignia, the portrait, and the dossier watermark as a single repeated motif), the 3am filing timestamp, the single-name signature, the absent Unique-rank creator as an unspoken origin footnote useful for later Unique-operator content, fashion-press covers that mention the player's guild as a competitor in the second paragraph, and sponsor meetings scheduled 'one more time' that extend indefinitely. Avoid villain framing: Vesper is warm, attentive, and does not target the player; the pressure is market-structural. Avoid also the motherly / empathetic AI register (Her, HAL nursing-bot lane) and the horror / surveillance-AI register — this rival is seductive-charismatic specifically, and tonal drift toward either adjacent lane should be rewritten.",
